---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import NetworkViewer from '../components/islands/NetworkViewer.astro';
import NetworkCharts from '../components/NetworkCharts.astro';
import data from '../data/glass3d.json';
import { binaryQn } from '../scripts/network.js';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const run = (id: string) => data.runs.find((r) => r.id === id)!.summary;
const pct = (v: number) => `${Math.round(v * 100)}%`;
const sf = run('silica-fast'), ss = run('silica-slow'), nf = run('soda-fast'), ns = run('soda-slow');
const ringRange = (s: typeof sf) => Object.keys(s.rings).map(Number);
const binary = binaryQn(data.glasses.soda.x);
---
<Base title="Network lab" description="Three thousand atoms of real silicate glass, melted and quenched in a molecular-dynamics simulation, to turn over and slice: the random network, its broken bridges, and its rings.">
  <article class="concept nv-page">
    <p class="detail-type">a simulation, run once and kept</p>
    <h1>Network lab</h1>
    <p class="summary">
      A cube of glass three and a half nanometers on a side: three thousand atoms of silicon, oxygen
      and, in one of the two glasses, sodium. They were melted and cooled in a research simulation,
      and their final positions are kept here so you can turn the block over and slice it. Unlike the
      disks of the <a href={`${base}/structure-lab/`}>Structure lab</a>, these atoms bond: every
      silicon holds four oxygens at the corners of a tetrahedron, and the tetrahedra join corner to
      corner into a network with no repeating pattern.
    </p>

    <NetworkViewer />

    <div class="prose-col">
      <h2>What to look for</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Silica, a thin slice.</strong> Almost every silicon is inked black: it shares all four
        of its oxygens with neighbors, which chemists write Q⁴ ({pct(ss.qn[4])} of them in the slower
        quench). Almost every oxygen is a bridge between two silicons. This is the picture W. H. Zachariasen drew in 1932, in two dimensions, before anyone
        could check it: a network as connected as a crystal's, with the order taken out. X-ray
        diffraction soon backed it, and the best of those measurements (Mozzi and Warren, 1969) found
        the angle at each bridging oxygen spread from 120° all the way to 180°, most often near 144°.
        In this simulated silica the commonest angle is {Math.round(ss.angle.peak)}° (to the nearest
        5°) and the mean {Math.round(ss.angle.mean)}°, the spread runs as wide, and {pct(ss.siCoord[4])} of the silicons have exactly four
        oxygens.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Rings.</strong> Follow bridges from one silicon around and back to it and you trace a
        ring. In cristobalite, the crystal silica forms most easily, every smallest ring has six
        tetrahedra. Turn on the rings and step through the sizes: the glass has rings from
        {Math.min(...ringRange(ss))} to {Math.max(...ringRange(ss))} tetrahedra, most of them six or
        seven. The variety of rings is what a crystal cannot have, and it is one way to
        say exactly what "disordered" means for a network whose every atom still has its proper
        neighbors.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Soda silica.</strong> Switch to the glass with sodium, which has the proportions of
        Na₂O·3SiO₂, a quarter of the oxide units soda. Each Na₂O brings one oxygen and two sodium
        ions. The extra oxygen cannot join the network as a bridge; instead one Si–O–Si bridge is
        cut, and the two silicons each keep an oxygen that now hangs free, drawn open here. The
        sodium ions, dashed rings, sit beside those loose oxygens, each surrounded by about
        {nf.naO.toFixed(0)} of them, and hold the charge in balance. Cutting the network this way is
        why soda lowers the melting temperature so far.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the breaks fall</h2>
      <p>
        Counting is enough to say how many bridges must break: in Na₂O·3SiO₂ there are two loose
        oxygens for every three silicons. It does not say how they are shared out. The simplest
        guess, the binary model, puts at most one loose oxygen on each tetrahedron, which makes the
        glass {pct(binary[3])} Q³ and {pct(binary[4])} Q⁴. Nuclear magnetic resonance of ²⁹Si can
        tell the Qⁿ species apart, and measurements of real sodium silicates (Maekawa and colleagues,
        1991) find something close to the binary model with a little of the reaction
        2Q³ ⇌ Q² + Q⁴ mixed in. The simulations spread the breaks much more widely:
      </p>
      <NetworkCharts chart="qn" />
      <p class="caption">
        Qⁿ is the number of a silicon's four oxygens that bridge to another silicon. The NMR row and
        the 2.25 K/ps simulation by the authors of the potential are read from their figure 5
        (Sundararaman et al., 2019), so they are good to a few percent.
      </p>
      <p>
        The simulated glass has {pct(nf.qn[2])} Q² in the fast quench and {pct(ns.qn[2])} in the
        slower one, close to what the potential's authors found at a rate four times slower still; the
        measured glass has almost none. The usual explanation, which those authors give, is the cooling
        rate. A liquid's structure is more disordered the hotter it is, and a glass keeps the structure
        of the liquid at the temperature where it stopped rearranging. A simulation cools so fast that
        it stops rearranging hundreds of degrees hotter than a real glass, and keeps that hotter
        liquid's disorder. Cooling tenfold slower moves it only a little here: the gap to the furnace is a factor of
        10¹⁴, and a factor of ten is a small step along it.
      </p>

      <h2>Simulation is fast, the furnace is slow</h2>
      <p>
        The two quench rates here are 100 and 10 kelvin per picosecond: 10¹⁴ and 10¹³ kelvin per
        second. A lehr anneals glass at a few kelvin per minute, about 10⁻¹ K/s, so the gap is some
        fourteen orders of magnitude. Simulations cannot close it: the slower runs here took over two
        hours each on one processor for a few hundredths of a nanosecond of glass history, and each
        tenfold slower cooling costs ten times as long. What they can do is show the trend. Vollmayr,
        Kob and Binder (1996) cooled simulated silica at a range of rates and found the glass
        transition falling with the logarithm of the rate, and the density, the ring sizes and the
        bond angles all shifting with it, the atomic structure more than the bulk properties.
      </p>
      <NetworkCharts chart="cooling" />
      <p class="caption">
        Density of each run as it was cooled, averaged over every picosecond: silica from 4000 K at one
        atmosphere, soda silica from 3000 K with the pressure let down from 1,000 atmospheres. The short bars at the left are the measured room-temperature densities: the
        medians of SciGlass entries for pure silica (2.20 g/cm³, 258 glasses) and for sodium silicates
        at 25 mol% Na₂O (2.43 g/cm³, 100 glasses).
      </p>
      <p>
        Read the curves from the right. Liquid silica densifies as it cools until, near 3000 K, the
        network stops rearranging and the curve flattens: that is the glass transition at simulation
        speed, far above the real one near 1460 K (1190 °C, the median of 16 SciGlass entries). Below
        it the glass hardly contracts at all, which is real silica's famous near-zero expansion. The
        soda silica's curves are harder to read. Its liquid boils at 4000 K and one atmosphere in this
        model, so, as the potential's authors did, it was melted at the measured glass density and
        cooled from 3000 K at a pressure let down from 1,000 atmospheres to one along the way. The
        falling pressure lets the glass expand as it cools and hides the bend; the curves show only
        that the faster quench ends less dense, {nf.density.toFixed(2)} against
        {ns.density.toFixed(2)} g/cm³, and that the slower one lands on the measured 2.43. The ring
        sizes respond to the rate too, and to the sodium:
      </p>
      <NetworkCharts chart="rings" />
      <p class="caption">
        Rings per silicon atom, by the number of tetrahedra in the ring. A ring is counted for each
        silicon and each pair of its bridges as the shortest closed path through them (King, 1967),
        and each distinct ring once. The slower silica has fewer of the strained three- and
        four-membered rings and more six-membered ones, the direction Vollmayr, Kob and Binder found;
        in the soda silica, where bridges are cut, rings of six and seven are rarer and rings of nine and ten, and longer, far commoner.
      </p>

      <h2>How this was computed</h2>
      <p>
        Each glass is a run of LAMMPS, the molecular-dynamics code from Sandia, with the SHIK
        potential (Sundararaman, Huang, Ispas and Kob, 2018 and 2019). SHIK treats the atoms as
        charged spheres: silicon +{data.potential.charges.Si} e, sodium +{data.potential.charges.Na} e
        and oxygen whatever balances them, smaller than the textbook ionic charges, because the bond is
        partly covalent. Between each pair it adds a short-range repulsion and a weak attraction
        (the Buckingham form) plus a steep wall that stops atoms falling into each other at high
        temperature. Its parameters were fitted so that a simulated liquid matches one computed from
        quantum mechanics and the glass matches the measured density and stiffness. Nothing in it
        says "make tetrahedra"; the tetrahedra form because the charges and sizes favor them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each run started from atoms scattered at random in a box and melted at 6000 K. Silica was
        settled at 4000 K and one atmosphere; the soda silica at 3000 K, first at the measured glass
        density and then at 1,000 atmospheres, because at one atmosphere and 4000 K the model liquid
        boils away. Each was then cooled in a straight line to 300 K, the soda silica's pressure
        falling to one atmosphere as it went, and held there for 30 picoseconds. The two runs of each glass start from the same liquid and differ only in the
        cooling rate. The analysis on this page, which atoms are bonded, the Qⁿ counts and the rings,
        is computed from the stored positions by this site's own code, with a bond being any Si–O pair
        closer than 2.2 Å. The input files and the analysis are in the repository, so every number
        here can be rerun.
      </p>

      <h2>What the model leaves out</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The cooling rate.</strong> Fourteen orders of magnitude too fast, as above. The Q² excess and the high glass transition are the visible cost.</li>
        <li><strong>Electrons.</strong> The charges are fixed numbers, and the atoms cannot polarize or share electrons differently from place to place. A loose oxygen near a sodium is really more negative than a bridging one; here they carry the same charge.</li>
        <li><strong>Size.</strong> Three thousand atoms in a box 3.5 nanometers wide that repeats in every direction. Anything larger than the box, such as sodium-rich channels on the scale Greaves (1985) proposed for alkali silicates, may not form properly in it.</li>
        <li><strong>Statistics.</strong> One sample per glass and rate. Another random start would shift the percentages by a point or two; the authors of the potential averaged four samples of ten thousand atoms.</li>
        <li><strong>The long-range forces.</strong> The electrostatic forces are cut off at 1 nanometer with Wolf's method, which the potential was fitted with, rather than summed over the whole repeating lattice.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Each glass is a model: what a particular, published approximation to the forces between
        atoms produces when cooled impossibly fast. Where it can be checked, against density, the
        bond angles and NMR, it is close, and it is wrong in the direction its fast quench predicts.
      </p>

      <section class="sources">
        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>S. Sundararaman, L. Huang, S. Ispas and W. Kob, New optimization scheme to obtain interaction potentials for oxide glasses, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1063/1.5023707" rel="noopener">J. Chem. Phys. 148, 194504 (2018)</a>: the SHIK potential for silica.</li>
          <li>S. Sundararaman, L. Huang, S. Ispas and W. Kob, New interaction potentials for alkali and alkaline-earth aluminosilicate glasses, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1063/1.5079663" rel="noopener">J. Chem. Phys. 150, 154505 (2019)</a> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.01284" rel="noopener">arXiv:1811.01284</a>): the sodium parameters and charges, and the Qⁿ comparison; LAMMPS pair style from <a href="https://github.com/compmatscirpi/SHIK-potential" rel="noopener">compmatscirpi/SHIK-potential</a>.</li>
          <li>A. P. Thompson et al., LAMMPS: a flexible simulation tool for particle-based materials modeling at the atomic, meso, and continuum scales, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cpc.2021.108171" rel="noopener">Comput. Phys. Commun. 271, 108171 (2022)</a>.</li>
          <li>H. Maekawa, T. Maekawa, K. Kawamura and T. Yokokawa, The structural groups of alkali silicate glasses determined from ²⁹Si MAS-NMR, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-3093(91)90400-Z" rel="noopener">J. Non-Cryst. Solids 127, 53–64 (1991)</a>.</li>
          <li>W. H. Zachariasen, The atomic arrangement in glass, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1021/ja01349a006" rel="noopener">J. Am. Chem. Soc. 54, 3841–3851 (1932)</a>.</li>
          <li>R. L. Mozzi and B. E. Warren, The structure of vitreous silica, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1107/S0021889869006868" rel="noopener">J. Appl. Cryst. 2, 164–172 (1969)</a>: Si–O 1.62 Å, Si–O–Si angles from 120° to 180° with the maximum at 144°.</li>
          <li>S. V. King, Ring configurations in a random network model of vitreous silica, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/2131112a0" rel="noopener">Nature 213, 1112–1113 (1967)</a>: the ring definition used here.</li>
          <li>K. Vollmayr, W. Kob and K. Binder, Cooling-rate effects in amorphous silica: a computer-simulation study, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.54.15808" rel="noopener">Phys. Rev. B 54, 15808 (1996)</a>.</li>
          <li>G. N. Greaves, EXAFS and the structure of glass, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-3093(85)90289-3" rel="noopener">J. Non-Cryst. Solids 71, 203–217 (1985)</a>: the modified random network.</li>
          <li>SciGlass, through D. R. Cassar's plain-text conversion (<a href="https://github.com/drcassar/SciGlass" rel="noopener">drcassar/SciGlass</a>): the measured densities.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
      <p class="back"><a href={`${base}/labs/`}>← all the labs</a></p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
<style>
  .nv-page { max-width: 72rem; }
  .nv-page .summary { max-width: 46rem; }
  .prose-col { max-width: 46rem; }
  .caption { font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--ink-3); margin-top: 0; }
</style>
